 "literal": "Sharp clawed field turtle / white belly having seen resembling / tight bound new leather cover / clear-headed great kinai drum playing always / enmity took those fortress wall battering down / dust smeared fragrant cheeks / beautiful spots thick necks / different different spreading moving / king possessing defensive forest nearby spreading / uplifted tusk dark rough-skinned broad trunk / perfected work many bull elephants / enemy land kings bowing tribute having given your / desiring poets poverty removing / greatly may they shine his red feet indeed saying / I if sing very good saying / many other blessings to praise those who were present their king / to reach pleasant palace wide front gate / perfected heroic anklet red feet approaching desiring / having conquered sounding victorious he of the drum / my lowliness due to despisingly he did not look / his greatness due to worthiness looking at / hill resembling male elephant shall I say? / trimmed mane horse shall I say? / courtyard filling herd of cattle shall I say? / house farmworkers with threshing floor shall I say? / there those gifts dream as to be bewildered quickly in reality / he who bestowed joyful abundant lord / for eons may you live of the Poozhi people great leader / rough tusked elephant battle mighty strong effort / wealthy Kadungo may you live Yathan / hostile enemies high umbrella having bowed this man / they will release indeed for long? not staying / small leaved Vanchi outer wall dashing against / roaring Porunai sand more than there / many villages surrounding paddy fields / all growing rice more than many indeed",
 "translation": "Playing always the clear-headed, great kinai drum, covered with new leather tightly bound, resembling the white belly of a sharp-clawed field turtle, I praise you. Your many bull elephants, with perfected skills, batter down the fortress walls of those who oppose you, their fragrant cheeks smeared with dust, their thick necks covered with beautiful spots, spreading out in different directions, roaming near the defensive forests of enemy kings. When enemy kings bow and pay tribute, you remove the poverty of the desiring poets. When I sang, 'May his red feet shine greatly,' the others present said, 'Very good!' and praised you with many other blessings. Desiring to approach the red feet adorned with perfected heroic anklets of the king who possesses the victorious drum sounding of conquest, I stood at the wide front gate of his pleasant palace. He did not look at me with contempt due to my lowliness, but looked at me with worthiness due to his own greatness. Shall I call his gifts a male elephant resembling a hill? Shall I call them horses with trimmed manes? Shall I call them herds of cattle filling the courtyard? Shall I call them threshing floors filled with farmworkers? He, the joyful and abundant lord, bestowed all these upon me in reality so quickly that I was bewildered, wondering if it was a dream. May you live for eons, O great leader of the Poozhi people, wealthy Kadungo Vazhi Yathan, whose strong efforts are mighty in battle with rough-tusked elephants! Hostile enemies will surely bow their high umbrellas and yield to him. May your years of life be more than the sands of the roaring Porunai river that dashes against the outer walls of Vanchi with its small leaves, and more than all the grains of rice growing in the paddy fields surrounding many villages!",
 "modern": "கூர்மையான நகங்களை உடைய வயல் ஆமையின் வெண்மையான வயிற்றைப் போன்ற, புதிய தோல் போர்த்தப்பட்ட கிணைப் பறையை எப்போதும் இசைத்துக் கொண்டு, பகைவர்களின் மதில்களை இடித்துத் துகளாக்கிய, மதம் பொழியும் கன்னங்களையும் புள்ளிகள் நிறைந்த கழுத்தையும் உடைய யானைகளைக் கொண்டு பகை மன்னர்களின் காவல் காடுகளை அழித்து, அவர்கள் தரும் திறையைக் கொண்டு பாணர்களின் வறுமையைப் போக்கும் சேரமானே! உன்னுடைய செவ்வடிகள் வாழ்க என்று நான் வாழ்த்தியபோது, அங்கிருந்தோர் அதைப் பாராட்டிப் பல்லாண்டு வாழ்த்தினர். வெற்றி முரசினைக் கொண்ட உனது அரண்மனை வாயிலில் உன்னைத் தரிசிக்க நின்ற என் வறுமையைக் கண்டு நீ இகழவில்லை. உனது பெருந்தன்மையால் எனக்குக் குன்று போன்ற யானைகளையும், குதிரைகளையும், மன்று நிறைந்த பசுக்கூட்டங்களையும், நெற்களங்களையும் வாரி வழங்கினாய். விழித்தெழும் முன்னே கனவோ என்று நான் மருளும்படி நனவிலேயே இவற்றைத் தந்த வள்ளலே! பூழியர் பெருமகனே, செல்வக் கடுங்கோ வாழியாதனே! உனது பகைவர்கள் உன்னிடம் பணிந்து திறை தருவர். சிறிய இலைகளைக் கொண்ட வஞ்சி மாநகரின் புறமதிலைத் தாக்கும் பொருநை ஆற்றின் மணலை விடவும், பல ஊர்களைச் சூழ்ந்த கழனிகளில் விளையும் நெல்மணிகளை விடவும் பல ஆண்டுகள் நீ டூழி வாழ்க!",
 "explanation": "This poem is a Puram song of the Pāṭāṇ tiṇai, where the poet Kundukat Pāliyāthanār praises the Chera king Selvak Kadungo Vāzhi Yāthan. The poet describes how he arrived at the king's palace gate playing his kinai drum, which is beautifully compared to the white belly of a sharp-clawed field turtle. Despite the poet's poverty and lowliness, the king treated him with immense dignity and showered him with magnificent gifts—elephants, horses, cattle, and fertile lands—so rapidly that the poet wondered if he was dreaming. The poem concludes with a grand blessing (vāḻttiyal), wishing the king a life longer than the sands of the roaring Porunai river that flows by the capital city of Vanchi, and more numerous than the grains of rice harvested from the surrounding fertile fields. Key words like 'நல்கியோனே' (he who bestowed) and 'வாழி' (may you live) highlight the themes of royal generosity and longevity.",
